  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 159 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nCooler air from Canada continues to cycle into Upper MI this morning \nas lake effect rain showers still occur the rest of today over \nmainly the eastern U.P. While some lightning may be seen at times \nthis morning in a couple of the lake effect showers, expect it to \nwrap up before noon as the instability in the lowest levels dwindles \nand high pressure ridging starts to move back into the area late \nthis week. With cooler air over the region now, expect temperatures \ncloser to normal for daytime highs today and even into Friday, only \ngetting into the 50s to mid 60s. \n\nExpect a return to well above normal temperatures this weekend as \nWAA moves into the region in association with a Clipper low \nsplitting and part of it traveling east along the U.S./Canada border \nwhile the other part traverses towards the Desert Southwest. Expect \nhighs on Saturday and Sunday to potentially soar above 80 degrees in \nthe interior areas, with lows only dropping into the 40s and 50s \novernight. In addition, with Hurricane Isaias moving through the \nSoutheast U.S. and potentially taking away Gulf moisture from our \narea, we could see fairly dry conditions which, in conjunction with \nthe strong winds associated with the WAA could bring elevated to \npotentially critical fire weather conditions across portions of \nwestern Upper MI on Saturday; fire weather partners and the public \nshould continue to monitor the forecast for fire weather concerns \nthis weekend as ongoing drought conditions will most likely \nexacerbate the problem. \n\nRainfall does look to eventually return to the region sometime early \nto mid next week as wrap-around moisture from a shortwave low and \nCAA moves overhead; we may see some snowflakes mix in at times if \nlake effect precipitation occurs. Once the cooler air returns next \nweek, expect temperatures to return back down to around normal for \nmid-October.",
